### What is LongAdder?

LongAdder is a high-throughput counter introduced in Java 8 that reduces CAS contention by distributing increments across an array of striped cells. Instead of all threads competing to update a single shared counter, each thread updates its own cell, and sum() adds them all up.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class LongAdderDemo {
    private static final LongAdder requestCounter = new LongAdder();

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
                requestCounter.increment();  // each thread increments its own cell
            }
        };
        Thread[] threads = new Thread[16];
        for (int i = 0; i < threads.length; i++) {
            threads[i] = new Thread(task, "Worker-" + i);
            threads[i].start();
        }
        for (Thread t : threads) t.join();
        System.out.println("Total requests: " + requestCounter.sum());
        // Always prints: 16000000
    }
}
```

### How does LongAdder work internally?

Under low contention, all increments go to a single `base` variable using CAS, identical to AtomicLong. When a CAS on `base` fails (indicating contention), the failing thread is assigned a Cell in the `cells[]` array based on its thread hash. Subsequent increments by that thread go directly to its Cell with minimal contention.

- Low contention (1-2 threads): CAS on base succeeds on first try. Performance equals AtomicLong.
- High contention (16+ threads): CAS on base fails frequently. Threads spread across cells. Each cell has minimal contention.
- sum() walks base + all cells: `base + cells[0].value + cells[1].value + ...`

### How is LongAdder different from AtomicLong?

| Feature | AtomicLong | LongAdder |
|---|---|---|
| Internal structure | Single volatile long | base + Cell[] array |
| get()/sum() | Exact current value | Eventually consistent sum |
| Low contention | Fast (single CAS) | Same (uses base only) |
| High contention | Slow (CAS retries) | 5-10x faster (striped cells) |
| Memory overhead | 16 bytes | Variable (cells grow) |
| incrementAndGet() | Returns exact new value | Not available (no exact get) |
| Use case | Exact real-time value | High-write counters/metrics |

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class ComparisonDemo {
    // For account balance: exact value needed
    private static final AtomicLong accountBalance = new AtomicLong(100000);

    // For request counter: exact real-time value NOT needed
    private static final LongAdder requestCount = new LongAdder();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // AtomicLong: exact value after operation
        long newBalance = accountBalance.addAndGet(-5000);
        System.out.println("Exact balance: " + newBalance);

        // LongAdder: fast increment, sum when needed
        requestCount.increment();
        requestCount.increment();
        long total = requestCount.sum();  // may not include in-flight increments
        System.out.println("Approximate count: " + total);
    }
}
```

### What is LongAccumulator?

LongAccumulator generalizes LongAdder with a custom accumulator function. Instead of summing, you can track maximums, minimums, or any associative, commutative function.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAccumulator;

public class LongAccumulatorDemo {
    private static final LongAccumulator maxTransaction =
            new LongAccumulator(Math::max, 0);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        maxTransaction.accumulate(2450);
        maxTransaction.accumulate(2500);
        maxTransaction.accumulate(2480);
        System.out.println("Max transaction: " + maxTransaction.get());  // 2500
    }
}
```

### When should you use LongAdder vs AtomicLong?

Use LongAdder when you need high write throughput and only read the total periodically (request counters, metrics dashboards, rate limiters). Use AtomicLong when you need an exact value after each operation (account balances, sequence numbers, resource counts).

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Why is sum() not atomic? Because reading base and all cells without a lock allows concurrent modifications during the traversal. Making it atomic would require locking, which defeats the purpose of avoiding contention.
- Can LongAdder go negative? Yes, decrement() subtracts 1. The semantics are the same as AtomicLong for arithmetic operations.
